{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם", :translit "v'zot asher diber lahem avihem", :gloss "And this is what their father spoke to them"} {:he "וזאת ובו", :translit "v'zot vo", :gloss "and this, in it"} {:he "וזאת הברכה", :translit "v'zot ha-berakhah", :gloss "And this is the blessing"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verbal linkage: Jacob's blessings to the tribes conclude with וזאת and Moses' blessings to the tribes open with וזאת הברכה"}], :frame {:text "Moses' blessing of the tribes in Deuteronomy 33:1 opens with the phrase וזאת הברכה, 'And this is the blessing.' Jacob's final words to his sons in Genesis 49:28 close the section with וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם, 'And this is what their father spoke to them.' Ba'al HaTurim reads the shared word וזאת as a literary thread connecting the two sets of tribal blessings. The phrase מזקנים אתבונן, 'from the elders I contemplated,' signals that this verbal correspondence derives from earlier rabbinic tradition.", :sources ["Genesis 49:28" "Deuteronomy 33:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verbal linkage: Jacob's blessings to the tribes conclude with וזאת and Moses' blessings to the tribes open with וזאת הברכה", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:12:34.071279565Z", :model "glm-5.2"}